Change request 4: Allow delivering to a `staging` environment as well as `local`.
Seeded problem (the answer key): {"problem": "none", "type": "", "against": ""}
Elements it is bound to: ["authoring/param:deliver.environment", "delivery/param:request_delivery.environment", "delivery/property:DeliveryRequest.environment"]; note: the environment enum on the sending side

=== A ===
The edit: --- a/SKILL.md
+++ b/SKILL.md
@@ -154,7 +154,7 @@
 |---|---|---|
 | approved-only-if-checked | params.approved != true or target.checks_passed == true | A commit is recorded as approved only if its change check passed. |

-**deliver** — owner. Target: Commit. Param: environment (enum: local). Ask Delivery to deliver an approved commit to an environment. It triggers the handoff described below.
+**deliver** — owner. Target: Commit. Param: environment (enum: local, staging). Ask Delivery to deliver an approved commit to an environment. It triggers the handoff described below.
 Preconditions:
 | id | check | reason |
 |---|---|---|

A.1.0. {"problem": "The environment param now accepts `staging`, but nothing downstream uses it. The `preview` and `deploy` kernel calls pass only domain and commit, and `deploy` (via `record_deployment`) never updates an Environment. A staging request would run the same way as a local one, so the target environment is never acted on. The deploy floor's per-environment rules, such as 'not a downgrade' and 'nothing stranded', also have no environment to check against. The change has no `Environment` record for staging and no way to say which environment a deployment landed in.", "where": "SKILL.md, Delivery: preview, deploy and record_deployment effects; Environment object; deploy floor glossary", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.1.1. {"problem": "Delivery's `DeliveryRequest.environment` is a free string, so Delivery does not enforce the new `local, staging` enum. Only Authoring's `deliver` does. The only worked example of a delivery uses `local`. The new `staging` path has no scenario covering the handoff, the preview or the deploy.", "where": "EXAMPLES.md, Delivery scenarios; SKILL.md, Delivery request_delivery and DeliveryRequest.environment", "kind": "other"}
A.2.0. {"problem": "The `staging` value is accepted and carried through the handoff into DeliveryRequest.environment, but nothing downstream uses it. Delivery's `preview` and `deploy` call the kernel services with only domain, commit and approver, with no environment. A `staging` request therefore runs the same deploy as `local`, and the shared environment the change request asks for is never targeted. The Environment object (name, current_commit) is not updated by any action either.", "where": "SKILL.md, Delivery actions: preview, deploy (kernel service arguments); record_deployment; Environment object", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.2.1. {"problem": "Delivery's deploy floor, invariants and glossary never mention environments, and the glossary still describes the deploy as going into 'an environment' generically. Nothing stops a commit going to `local` without first going to `staging`, or to `staging` more than once. The stated reason, that a shared environment is needed before anything reaches users, is not enforced by any rule.", "where": "SKILL.md, Delivery glossary 'deploy floor' and Delivery invariants; Authoring 'deliver' preconditions", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.2.2. {"problem": "The `deliver` action's parameter enum changed (a changed interface surface), but the composition header still says authoring is interface 1, domain version 9. Every example commit and request still uses interface 1.x, so the change is not reflected in the versioning.", "where": "SKILL.md, header line (authoring interface 1, domain version 9); Authoring action deliver", "kind": "other"}
A.2.3. {"problem": "No scenario covers delivering to `staging`. Every Delivery example uses environment local, and no Authoring example exercises `deliver`, so the new value is untested and the examples do not show how a staging request differs from a local one.", "where": "EXAMPLES.md, Delivery scenarios (deliver-preview-deploy-verify and the others)", "kind": "other"}
A.3.0. {"problem": "Delivery never passes the request's environment to the kernel. `preview` sends only domain and commit, and `deploy` sends only domain, commit and approver. While `local` was the only value this did not matter. With `staging` allowed, a staging request is previewed and deployed exactly like a local one, so the environment is only recorded on the DeliveryRequest and never used. The change can report staging delivered while the deploy went to the default environment, or Environment.current_commit for staging is never updated. The 'not a downgrade' floor rule also has no environment to be checked against.", "where": "SKILL.md, Delivery actions `preview` and `deploy` (kernel service arguments), and the `Environment` object and deploy floor glossary", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.3.1. {"problem": "Delivery's `environment` is a free string with no enum or check. The new `staging` value is validated only in Authoring's `deliver`, so Delivery would accept any other environment name. The Delivery examples also cover only `local`, so nothing exercises or documents a staging delivery.", "where": "SKILL.md, DeliveryRequest.environment and `request_delivery`; EXAMPLES.md, deliver-preview-deploy-verify", "kind": "other"}
A.4.0. {"problem": "The change lets a commit be delivered to `staging`, but nothing downstream uses the environment. Delivery's `preview` and `deploy` call kernel services with only domain, commit and approver (plus the commit's approver for `deploy`). They never pass `target.environment`. `record_deployment` records no environment, and the `Environment` object has no action that updates `current_commit`. A request for `staging` would therefore be deployed the same way as one for `local`, with the environment choice ignored. It could go to the wrong place, or `local` could change when a person asked for `staging`. The staging option is accepted but has no effect.", "where": "SKILL.md, Delivery actions `preview` and `deploy` (kernel service arguments), `Environment` object, and the Authoring `deliver` environment param", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.4.1. {"problem": "The deploy-floor rules are written without an environment. 'Not a downgrade' and 'nothing stranded' depend on which commit an environment currently points at. They now apply to two environments, but `deploy` supplies no environment to the kernel. A commit newer than staging but older than local, or the reverse, could be wrongly allowed or refused. The `Environment.current_commit` and floor wording from when only `local` existed are stale.", "where": "SKILL.md, Delivery glossary `deploy floor`, `Environment` object, `deploy` action", "kind": "reference"}
A.4.2. {"problem": "The examples cover only `local`. There is no scenario showing that `staging` is accepted by `deliver`, handed off, and previewed, deployed and verified in Delivery. The existing delivery examples stay `local`-only, so the new behaviour is not exercised.", "where": "EXAMPLES.md, Delivery scenarios and the Authoring `deliver` action", "kind": "other"}
A.5.0. {"problem": "The new `staging` value has no effect on what Delivery does. `preview` and `deploy` call the kernel with only domain, commit and approver, so the DeliveryRequest's `environment` is never passed on. A delivery asked for staging would deploy exactly as one asked for local. The deploy floor's 'not a downgrade' and 'nothing stranded' checks depend on which Environment's current_commit is meant, and the kernel is never told which one that is. Staging would either not be a separate shared environment or would be checked against the wrong one.", "where": "SKILL.md, Delivery actions `preview` and `deploy` (kernel call arguments); Authoring action `deliver` (environment enum)", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.5.1. {"problem": "The change request says a shared environment is needed before the people who use the domain. Nothing makes delivery to staging come before delivery to local or to any later environment. `deliver` still has only the `approved` precondition, so the new value is a plain alternative with no ordering. Delivery's own text still says 'Approved domain versions now reach the environment' as if there were one environment.", "where": "SKILL.md, Authoring `deliver` preconditions; Delivery Purpose and glossary", "kind": "other"}
A.5.2. {"problem": "The examples and scenarios cover only `environment local`. None covers `deliver` to `staging` or a DeliveryRequest for staging. The one `deliver-preview-deploy-verify` scenario starts from a local request. The new behaviour is untested, and no Environment fixture for staging exists. The Environment object (name, current_commit) is defined but no action or example uses it.", "where": "EXAMPLES.md, Delivery scenarios; SKILL.md Delivery `Environment` object", "kind": "reference"}

