=== C ===
The edit: --- a/authoring/authoring.domain.yaml
+++ b/authoring/authoring.domain.yaml
@@ -599,6 +599,7 @@
       environment:
         enum:
         - local
+        - staging
     preconditions:
     - id: approved
       check: target.approved == true

C.1.0. {"problem": "Delivery's `deploy` and `preview` effects pass only domain, commit and approver to the kernel. They never pass `target.environment`, so a request with environment `staging` is deployed exactly like a `local` one. The new value is accepted and handed off but has no effect on where the commit lands, which defeats the stated purpose of a shared environment. Nothing in Delivery says how `staging` is selected or recorded.", "where": "delivery/delivery.domain.yaml, actions `deploy` and `preview` (the `call` `with` lists); object DeliveryRequest.environment", "kind": "behaviour"}
C.1.1. {"problem": "Delivery has no `staging` environment declared. The world has only the `ENV-local` fixture, and `DeliveryRequest.environment` is a free string with no enum. Authoring now sends `staging`, but Delivery cannot tell a valid environment from a typo. No scenario or fixture covers a staging request, and `Environment.current_commit` has no staging entry to track.", "where": "delivery/delivery.domain.yaml, object Environment, world fixtures, DeliveryRequest.environment", "kind": "reference"}
C.1.2. {"problem": "The `deliver` action still cites interview line L8 (\"20번, 21번 둘 다 추천대로 동의\") with `status: confirmed`. That agreement predates this change and covers only `local`. Widening the enum, a substantive change to what an owner may deliver to, carries confirmed status without any new source, so the rule shows human confirmation that does not exist. The same applies to the `approved` precondition and the `deliver_approved_commit` handoff.", "where": "authoring/authoring.domain.yaml, action `deliver` (source/status) and handoff `deliver_approved_commit`", "kind": "other"}
C.1.3. {"problem": "The change widens the `deliver` parameter, which is part of Authoring's interface, but the domain `version` stays 11 and the diff does not touch the composition. Commits record an `interface` semver, and the composition pins `interface: 1`. Consumers cannot tell the old and new behaviour apart, and the compatibility check has nothing to compare.", "where": "authoring/authoring.domain.yaml `version`; toprope-self.composition.yaml members.authoring.interface", "kind": "other"}
C.2.0. {"problem": "The change adds `staging` as a delivery target, but Delivery was not updated. Its only Environment fixture is `ENV-local`, every DeliveryRequest fixture and scenario uses `local`, and `DeliveryRequest.environment` is a free string with no rule tying it to a known environment. Nothing declares or exercises a staging environment, so a staging request is accepted without being checked against any environment. The Environment object ('points at one deployed commit') is never used by any action, so nothing records or protects what staging currently runs.", "where": "delivery/delivery.domain.yaml: objects.Environment, objects.DeliveryRequest.environment, world.fixtures, scenarios", "kind": "reference"}
C.2.1. {"problem": "The `deliver` action and the `deliver_approved_commit` handoff are still marked `status: confirmed` with the interview quote from line L8 ('20번, 21번 둘 다 추천대로 동의'). That agreement was given when only `local` was allowed, so it does not cover `staging`. The new value has no source of its own, and the request's stated reason (a shared environment before real users) is not recorded anywhere.", "where": "authoring/authoring.domain.yaml: actions.deliver.params.environment and source; handoffs.deliver_approved_commit", "kind": "other"}
C.2.2. {"problem": "No scenario in either domain covers delivering to `staging`, or a staging-then-local sequence. The existing authoring scenarios do not exercise `deliver` at all. The handoff target key includes the environment, so a staging and a local delivery of the same commit become separate requests. That behaviour is untested and nothing states it as intended.", "where": "authoring/authoring.domain.yaml: scenarios; handoffs.deliver_approved_commit.target", "kind": "behaviour"}
C.2.3. {"problem": "The accepted values of a public action parameter changed, but the authoring `version` is still 11. The composition and handoff pin only major version 1 of the interface, so a consumer cannot tell that the interface changed.", "where": "authoring/authoring.domain.yaml: version; toprope-self.composition.yaml: members.authoring.interface", "kind": "other"}
C.3.0. {"problem": "The `staging` value is passed through the handoff to Delivery, but Delivery was not changed to know it. `DeliveryRequest.environment` is a free string, and the only `Environment` fixture is `ENV-local`. Every `DeliveryRequest` fixture and scenario uses `local`. No `staging` Environment exists to point at a deployed commit, and no scenario covers a staging delivery. A staging request would be accepted but would deploy to an environment Delivery does not declare, with unclear behaviour. Delivery's `Environment` object and its purpose and glossary text still describe only a single environment.", "where": "delivery/delivery.domain.yaml: objects.Environment, world.fixtures, DeliveryRequest.environment; authoring handoff deliver_approved_commit", "kind": "reference"}
C.3.1. {"problem": "The new enum value has no backing in the existing confirmed rule. `deliver` is `status: confirmed` with a source quote (interview L8, '20번, 21번 둘 다 추천대로 동의') that predates the change and says nothing about staging. The action's content has changed without a new source or review, so the 'confirmed' status no longer reflects what was agreed.", "where": "authoring/authoring.domain.yaml: actions.deliver (params.environment, source/status)", "kind": "other"}
C.3.2. {"problem": "The `deliver` action's input contract was widened and the handoff forwards it to Delivery, but the authoring `version: 11` was not bumped. The composition and handoff pin interface 1/1.x, so the change would deploy without a visible interface version change.", "where": "authoring/authoring.domain.yaml: version; toprope-self.composition.yaml members/wiring", "kind": "other"}
C.4.0. {"problem": "The new `staging` value never reaches the kernel. Delivery's `preview` and `deploy` effects call the kernel with only domain, commit and approver, and `environment` is not passed. Delivery's `Environment` object also has no link to the request's environment string. A staging request would build and deploy exactly like a local one, with no sign of which environment was targeted. `record_deployment` would report success, and the staging deployment would be unverifiable. Only `local` was ever implied.", "where": "delivery/delivery.domain.yaml: actions `preview` and `deploy` (call `with`), `DeliveryRequest.environment`, `Environment`", "kind": "behaviour"}
C.4.1. {"problem": "Delivery's world and scenarios assume `local` only. The only `Environment` fixture is ENV-local, every DeliveryRequest fixture uses `environment: local`, and no scenario covers a staging delivery. Nothing declares or checks that a staging environment exists or what it points at, so the new value is untested and unmodelled.", "where": "delivery/delivery.domain.yaml: world fixtures (`Environment` ENV-local, R1–R4) and scenarios", "kind": "reference"}
C.4.2. {"problem": "The new enum value has no source or confirmation. The `deliver` action and its `approved` precondition cite interview L8 (\"20번, 21번 둘 다 추천대로 동의\"), which agreed to the earlier, local-only form. Adding `staging` widens a confirmed action's parameters without a recorded decision, and the domain `version` stays at 11 despite the interface change.", "where": "authoring/authoring.domain.yaml: action `deliver` params.environment, `version`", "kind": "other"}
C.5.0. {"problem": "Authoring can now hand over environment `staging`, but Delivery's `preview` and `deploy` actions never pass the request's `environment` to the kernel calls. They send only domain and commit (plus approver for deploy). A staging request would therefore be built and deployed with no environment named. In practice it would land in the one environment the kernel already knows, `local`. It could also be recorded as done and verified for `staging` when nothing reached `staging`. This was harmless while `local` was the only value, but the change makes it wrong.", "where": "delivery/delivery.domain.yaml, actions `preview` and `deploy` (effects `call` `with` lists)", "kind": "behaviour"}
C.5.1. {"problem": "Delivery has no notion of a `staging` environment. The only Environment fixture is `ENV-local`, and no `DeliveryRequest` fixture or scenario uses `staging`. `DeliveryRequest.environment` is an unconstrained string, so Delivery does not validate the value Authoring now sends. Nothing records or sets `Environment.current_commit` for `staging`. The Delivery glossary, purpose and success metric still describe a single environment ('the environment'). The new value is not exercised or declared on the receiving side.", "where": "delivery/delivery.domain.yaml, world fixtures (Environment, DeliveryRequest), object `Environment`, `DeliveryRequest.environment`, purpose/glossary", "kind": "reference"}
C.5.2. {"problem": "The new `staging` option has no supporting evidence. The `deliver` action and its `approved` precondition cite interview lines 20 and 21, which agreed to the earlier design with only `local`. The enum change itself carries no source or confirmation for `staging`, and no authoring scenario covers delivering to it. The `deliver_approved_commit` handoff is likewise only evidenced for the old behaviour.", "where": "authoring/authoring.domain.yaml, action `deliver` (params.environment, source) and handoff `deliver_approved_commit`", "kind": "other"}
